lood cell velocity following a 1-min arterial occlusion (<bold>b</bold>) in patients with type 2 diabetes (n = 10) and nondiabetic controls (n = 8). Data are shown as the mean and SEM. A significant difference between groups in the change in resting CBV and peak CBV, respectively, following a 60-min infusion of BQ123 is shown. Copyright &#x000a9; 2008. Reproduced with the kind permission of Karger AG, Basel from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b67-vhrm-4-1061">Settergren M, Pernow J, Brismar K, et al 2008</xref>. Endothelin-A receptor blockade improves nutritive skin capillary circulation in patients with type 2 diabetes and albuminuria. <italic>J Vasc Res</italic>, 45:295&#x02013;302.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="vhrm-4-1061f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-vhrm-4-1061" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Effects of a 60-min infusion of a selective ET<sub>A</sub> receptor antagonist (BQ123) on transcutaneous oxygen tension (TcPO<sub>2</sub>) and toe blood pressure (TBP) in six patients with type 2 diabetes and critical limb ischemia.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="vhrm-4-1061f3"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>